1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:09,126
Japan, 1462. Boats carry rice, goods and people along the Yodo River, between Kyoto and the sea.

2
00:00:10,426 --> 00:00:16,723
Every stretch of river and road has owners: the court, the shogunate, temples, shrines and lords.

3
00:00:17,823 --> 00:00:22,430
Any of them may set a barrier across it, where everyone who passes pays a toll.

4
00:00:23,730 --> 00:00:33,303
That year, it's said, the Yodo alone had 380 barriers. On the road to the shrines at Ise, there were once sixty in eighteen kilometres.

5
00:00:34,803 --> 00:00:40,788
A load stopped and paid at every one, so goods arrived late, and dearer for every barrier they passed.

6
00:00:42,288 --> 00:00:47,396
Merchants and travellers needed a road they could use without paying every owner on it.

7
00:00:48,896 --> 00:00:53,864
Lords began clearing the barriers from their own lands, Oda Nobunaga among them.

8
00:00:54,964 --> 00:01:02,242
From about 1585, Toyotomi Hideyoshi forbade the tolls, and barriers across the country came down.

9
00:01:03,742 --> 00:01:14,200
Under the Tokugawa, the five roads ran under one set of rules, whoever's lands they crossed. In 1635, the lords were forbidden barriers of their own.

10
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:21,529
Each domain kept its own lord, its own laws and its own taxes. The road was what they shared.

11
00:01:23,229 --> 00:01:34,443
Travellers paid for what they used, a horse, a porter, a night at an inn, not every owner they passed. In 1771, more than two million pilgrims went to Ise.

12
00:01:36,143 --> 00:01:44,663
At the utility, Ravi finds barriers too: two data platforms, from two parts of the business, and a third product proposed to join them.

13
00:01:45,763 --> 00:01:51,431
Each brings its own permissions, policies and logs, so every rule would be kept three times.

14
00:01:52,531 --> 00:01:59,188
So Ravi proposes one platform: every domain owns its data on it, and every rule is kept once.

15
00:02:05,888 --> 00:02:10,833
Years ago, two parts of the utility each bought a data platform, from two vendors.

16
00:02:12,133 --> 00:02:20,726
One became the platform every other domain uses. The other is generation's, and holds the readings from its hydro and wind farms.

17
00:02:22,426 --> 00:02:31,749
Now the enterprise domain needs both, for one measure: the share of green-tariff customers' use, hour by hour, met by the utility's own farms.

18
00:02:33,849 --> 00:02:40,909
Its analysts propose a third product to join the two. It would query both where they are, so nothing would have to move.

19
00:02:43,009 --> 00:02:47,470
Ama asks for the measure in a ticket, and Ravi is asked how it should be built.

20
00:02:51,370 --> 00:02:54,827
Ravi draws what each one brings with it, side by side.

21
00:02:56,327 --> 00:03:06,107
Each platform has its own way to say who's who and who may see what, its own masks on private data, its own lineage, and its own log of who did what.

22
00:03:08,407 --> 00:03:10,727
The third product would bring a set of its own.

23
00:03:12,427 --> 00:03:20,641
So customers' meter readings, masked on the data platform, would be masked again in the third product, its own way, and checked in both.

24
00:03:22,741 --> 00:03:29,591
An analyst's rights would be granted in three places, three ways, and the measure's lineage would stop at every border.

25
00:03:31,691 --> 00:03:38,821
Every platform is a barrier with its own toll, and whoever crosses it pays: in rules kept again, and kept in step.

26
00:03:43,521 --> 00:03:51,892
Ravi keeps to one rule: the simplest architecture that does the job. Complexity nobody manages is what makes services fail.

27
00:03:53,592 --> 00:03:57,784
For most organisations, that means few components, on one platform.

28
00:03:59,084 --> 00:04:06,442
Many platforms, joined, fit only a few: a group spread over many countries, say, with a platform and a team in each.

29
00:04:07,942 --> 00:04:16,341
And moving to one is easier than it was: with open formats, and tools that convert most of the code, it can take months, not years.

30
00:04:20,041 --> 00:04:23,037
One platform doesn't mean one team owns everything.

31
00:04:24,137 --> 00:04:34,447
In a data mesh, as Zhamak Dehghani set it out, each domain owns its data, as products, on a self-serve platform, under rules the domains agree together.

32
00:04:36,347 --> 00:04:42,340
That self-serve platform is one platform, shared. Ownership is spread out; the platform isn't.

33
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:49,473
Done that way, a data mesh is often the simplest architecture: one platform, many owners.

34
00:04:50,973 --> 00:04:56,354
Done badly, it's the old teams with new names, and nobody in the business owning anything.

35
00:05:00,054 --> 00:05:04,050
Sometimes a second platform can't be helped, for a while or for good.

36
00:05:04,750 --> 00:05:13,166
A company just bought, until it moves. A separate legal entity that must keep its data apart. Data that must stay in its own country.

37
00:05:14,866 --> 00:05:20,596
Two things lessen the harm. Open table formats, so both platforms read the same files.

38
00:05:21,696 --> 00:05:26,516
And a catalogue that federates the other's, so people find all the data in one place.

39
00:05:27,816 --> 00:05:33,777
But each platform keeps its own permissions, policies and logs. The second set of rules stays.

40
00:05:35,477 --> 00:05:41,518
The shogunate kept some barriers too: about fifty checkpoints, there to keep order and check who passed.

41
00:05:43,018 --> 00:05:46,940
Keep a second platform the same way: only for a reason you can name.

42
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:55,417
Generation's platform has no such reason, so it moves onto the data platform.

43
00:05:56,517 --> 00:06:02,083
Generation keeps its data products: the same people, the same logic, the same owner.

44
00:06:03,383 --> 00:06:10,656
Its tables are already files in an open format, in the utility's own cloud storage, so their data needn't be copied.

45
00:06:12,156 --> 00:06:19,113
Until the move is done, the data platform's catalogue federates generation's, so Ama's measure can start at once.

46
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:29,381
A tool converts most of the jobs' code. The data team reviews each pull request, and compares every number with the old platform's for a month.

47
00:06:31,081 --> 00:06:37,156
Generation checks its own reports in test, and after four months the old platform is switched off.

48
00:06:41,056 --> 00:06:48,853
Now every rule is kept once. The mask on meter readings holds everywhere, and an analyst's rights are granted in one place.

49
00:06:50,553 --> 00:06:56,834
Ama's measure is built once, on one platform, with its lineage from each farm's readings to the number.

50
00:06:58,534 --> 00:07:07,171
One platform to pay for, not two, and no third. The two people who kept generation's platform running now build its data products.

51
00:07:11,071 --> 00:07:13,719
Here's the data practice, on Tomás's map.

52
00:07:15,019 --> 00:07:24,647
In the technology layer, generation's platform was a second node, with its own services to govern and secure: a second set of rules, in plain sight.

53
00:07:26,347 --> 00:07:32,809
In the application layer, generation's data products stayed generation's. Only what served them changed.

54
00:07:34,309 --> 00:07:40,879
Ravi's rule is a principle on the map: one platform, and a second only for a reason anyone can name.

55
00:07:42,579 --> 00:07:46,996
And the move was a work package, ending the day the old platform was switched off.

56
00:07:50,496 --> 00:07:57,990
Between Kuwana and Hinaga, where sixty barriers had stood, the Tōkaidō ran under the same rules as the whole road.

57
00:07:58,690 --> 00:08:04,028
At the utility, there's one platform now, and every domain still owns what it builds on it.

58
00:08:05,528 --> 00:08:07,716
Next: where it meets the applications.
